PART 1 — COMPLETE THEORY (NO CODE)
1. Definition

The FooBar Alternately problem requires two threads to print two different strings in a strict alternating order.

One thread prints:

foo

The other prints:

bar

The output must always be

foobarfoobarfoobar...

for exactly n repetitions.

2. Problem Statement

Given

n = 4

Create two threads.

Thread-1 prints

foo

Thread-2 prints

bar

Expected Output

foobarfoobarfoobarfoobar

or

foo
bar
foo
bar
foo
bar
foo
bar

depending on the printing format.

3. Why Is This Asked?

The interviewer wants to check whether you can synchronize two threads without relying on numeric conditions.

Unlike Odd–Even or Remainder problems, there is no shared counter used to decide whose turn it is.

The only thing that matters is:

Whose turn is it?

